-- title:
--   Regular functions have regular derivative with respect to a uniformiser
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra and $K$ perfect, and let $x \in F$ be such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$, that is, a valuation subring of $F$ which contains the image of $K$ under the structure map, is not the whole of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring; write $\operatorname{ord}_v$ for the associated integer-valued order function, the negative of the logarithm of the $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation attached to the height-one prime of $v$. Assume $t \in F$ satisfies $\operatorname{ord}_v t = 1$, i.e. $t$ is a uniformiser at $v$, and that $f \in F$ satisfies $\operatorname{ord}_v f \ge 0$. Suppose finally that $g \in F$ is such that in the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega^1_{F/K}$ one has $\mathrm{d}f = g \cdot \mathrm{d}t$. Then $\operatorname{ord}_v g \ge 0$. Thus differentiation with respect to a uniformiser at $v$ carries the valuation ring of $v$ into itself, the coefficient $g$ being given by the equation $\mathrm{d}f = g\,\mathrm{d}t$ rather than by a chosen coefficient function.
--
--   This is the statement that for a one-variable function field over a perfect constant field, $d/dt$ preserves the local ring $\mathcal{O}_v$ when $t$ is a local uniformiser at the place $v$; equivalently $\Omega^1$ is generated over $\mathcal{O}_v$ by $\mathrm{d}t$ on regular functions. It is used in the analysis of regularity, simple poles and residues of differentials at a place.
